WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.152
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.152 --> 00:00:03.360
DEBI CABRERA: Sorry I'm late.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.860
Thanks for letting me in.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.130
Good thing we're allowing
late data into our windows,

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.680
speaking of which, let's pick
up right where we left off,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.605
dealing with windows and
late data in Apache Beam.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.350
In the last video, we
looked at some sample data

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.080
and how Beam fits that
data into windows.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.650
With our example data, we set
up five-second fixed windows

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.750
and talked about how
some events might not

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.650
make it into the window if the
watermark has already passed.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:40.880
Data is considered late if it
comes in after Beam has already

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.270
processed the window.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.060
So what exactly does
that mean for your data?

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.680
The default behavior is to drop
late data, which means it's not

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.750
used in any data processing.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.240
Sometimes this might be
what you want, depending

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.470
on your business logic.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.270
However, if you
want your pipeline

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.630
to process data that arrives
after the watermark passes

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:03.260
the end of the window, you
can apply an allowed lateness

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.600
when you set up your windows.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.120
This option gives your
windows the chance

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.950
to react to late data.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.830
If allowed lateness is
set, the default trigger

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:15.650
will process the new data
and send out the results

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:16.890
immediately.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.630
So if we set the allowed
lateness to 10 seconds,

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.240
the final event from
our example will still

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.780
be processed because it's
less than 10 seconds late.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.520
The result of this
late processing

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.980
will be sent to the next
step in the pipeline.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.470
Beam uses watermarks
to provide an estimate

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:36.890
for knowing when no more data
is expected for a window.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.020
And allowed lateness
gives you the ability

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.690
to allow late data
into the windows.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:44.060
When we look at our example
data and the windows

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:48.410
after giving 10 seconds
for allowed lateness, well,

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.900
that's not quite what we
were expecting, is it?

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.810
It looks like there's two
windows with the same start

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:54.980
and end.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.380
That's because they're
actually the same window

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.110
but two different
window panes, which

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:03.920
is what we call it when a single
window aggregation is output

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.000
as two separate elements
for downstream processing.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.250
Depending on how you
want to handle data,

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:14.450
elements in a window might need
to be processed several times.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.850
The actual results from
processing a window

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:18.380
are called window panes.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:21.050
And they fall into
three categories--

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.350
early, on time, and late.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:27.740
Up until now, we've been dealing
with on-time panes, which

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.470
are the unique output that
contains all the received

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.650
data when Beam considers
the window complete

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.540
based on the watermark.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.120
But this new window
pane is actually late

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.950
because it has some
additional data that's

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:44.240
been allowed into the window,
thanks to allowed lateness.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.310
When a window has
multiple outputs,

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.970
Beam can handle passing on
the results in two ways.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.150
These two window panes,
one on time and one late,

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.300
have the same window properties,
like start and end time,

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.170
but not the same data.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.290
This happens when
the data is discarded

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.420
and Beam passes
along the new process

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.790
data as a separate window pane.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:07.260
If we don't want the
data to be discarded,

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.160
we can set the windows
to accumulate data

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.050
rather than discard it.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.240
Allowing accumulating
data gives us

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.610
this, where the
late window pane now

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.880
includes all of the data
from the first pane as well.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.070
Instead of just passing
along the new data,

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:25.250
beam will pass along
the entire aggregation

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.460
as a replacement for the
previous window pane.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.380
Ultimately, it's
your business logic

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.380
that will dictate which of these
is right for your pipeline.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.350
If you want to include late
data, deal with late data

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.810
differently, or
discard it entirely,

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:43.430
that's all possible through how
you set up the windows in Beam.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.700
With our example, the first
window having two panes

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.250
means each of those
panes would be sent down

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:53.000
the pipeline for whatever other
processing you have set up.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.920
One benefit to
these multiple panes

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.990
is that you can
actually process results

00:03:56.990 --> 00:04:00.530
before the late data comes in
and then process them again

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:01.970
with the late data.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:05.250
We've mentioned a few times
what Beam does by default,

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.400
such as the after
watermark trigger.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.380
But one of the properties
of the Beam model

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:12.230
which makes it so
powerful is how you can

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.360
configure all of the behaviors.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.519
Beam uses these
triggers to determine

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:20.399
when to send off the data from
a window through a window pane.

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:24.230
And there's lots of options
for how to configure them.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.930
Again, the watermark
is Beam's indicator

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.780
that there is no more
expected data for that window.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.570
The trigger for a window
is the actual mechanism

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:35.210
that sends off a window pane.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.390
After watermark is the default
trigger that's actually

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:40.550
responsible for
this, which will only

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:45.020
happen once per window after
the watermark has been cleared.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.240
That trigger will
happen no sooner

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:49.940
than the watermark, which
will then send off the window

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.040
pane for further processing.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.230
Depending on if you're
allowing late data,

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:57.860
events after the watermark
might go into the same window

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:01.850
and eventually become
a late window pane.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:05.000
But after watermark isn't
the only trigger you can use.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.050
Instead of our
small example data,

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:09.410
what if we were
collecting millions

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:13.310
of events over a large
window, like 24 hours?

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:15.390
Having the final
data be processed

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:19.380
after waiting the full 24 hours
is important for completeness.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:22.980
But that might increase how
long it takes to make decisions

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:24.510
based on that data.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:27.480
Thankfully, you can use
data-driven triggers

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.230
and processing time triggers
to get early results.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.360
Data-driven triggers
can take action

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.720
based on things like the
count of events in the window.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.750
So even if the
window is 24 hours,

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.200
you could trigger an early
window pane after the first 20

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.790
events are processed.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.490
In the same way, you can
use a processing time

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:51.150
trigger to get an early window
pane based on the processing

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.270
time.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.800
So if you want to see some early
results after only 12 hours

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.650
of processing, you can
use a processing time

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.530
trigger to get an early pane.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:02.910
But that's not all.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.520
Triggers can be
composed together

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.120
for more flexibility on
when you want a window pane.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.270
By using AfterAny, you can
group triggers together

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:15.300
so only one of the
subtriggers needs

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.230
to be met in order to tell Beam
that the window pane is ready.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:22.140
Another common case
is to use repeatedly

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.960
to have the trigger continue
to process the window

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.800
as new data arrives.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.790
If we take a look at
this code, this trigger

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:31.860
will happen every
time 20 events are

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.230
processed or after two hours.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.260
It's important to note
that these triggers can

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:40.920
be customized, but they only
set the minimum requirements

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.750
for sending out a window pane.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:45.090
Especially since Beam is
built to do distributed

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:48.480
data processing, there
might be a bit of extra time

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.880
or a few more events
than the minimum.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.070
Keep that in mind as you
experiment with triggers

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:55.750
and customize to fit your needs.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.660
So far, everything
we've talked about

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:01.260
is based on fixed windows that
have a start time and end time

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.000
and don't overlap.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.650
Even if you don't
declare a window,

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.730
Beam still uses
the global window,

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:09.420
which is great for batch
jobs but not so useful

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.090
when dealing with
unbounded data.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:14.190
If you want to use
windows that overlap,

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:16.950
you can use sliding windows.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.960
A good way to describe
sliding windows

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.720
is to think about
doing a moving average.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:24.480
If you wanted to get an
average of the number of events

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.420
in a 10-minute period
but wanted that average

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:31.480
to update every minute, sliding
windows would be a good fit.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:33.780
Since these windows
overlap, any events

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:35.790
in that overlapping
time period will

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.160
be processed in both windows.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:41.400
It's a good idea to understand
how intensive the event

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.800
processing is and
potentially perform

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:49.140
any processing before windowing
to avoid heavy resource usage.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:52.650
There's one more kind of
window, session windows.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:54.670
These windows are
a bit different.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:58.980
Instead of start and stop
times, you specify a gap time.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.810
This gap time is
how long a window

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:04.260
should wait without seeing
any activity before closing.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:07.560
Using the same example of
mobile device data from before,

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:11.040
we might use a session window
with a gap time of five minutes

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.260
to automatically
create windows based

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:15.390
on when the user is active.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:18.330
If the user doesn't have
any events for five minutes,

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:21.160
the window will close
and continue processing.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.990
So, to recap, windows
subdivide data.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:27.090
And Beam uses
triggers to send off

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:31.800
window panes, which could
be early, on time, or late.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:35.340
Allowed lateness can be used to
allow late data into a window

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.200
past the watermark.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.830
Windows can be fixed,
sliding, or session.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.080
And all of these
give you the ability

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:46.500
to really customize your
processing pipeline.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.060
Of course, this is a
lot of information,

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.215
and we are just
scratching the surface.

00:08:51.215 --> 00:08:52.590
There's a link in
the description

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:54.600
below to a detailed
notebook where

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.140
you can try out the code for
yourself and get hands-on.

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:00.000
I definitely recommend
you check it out

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.570
if you want to learn more.

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.560
Thanks for watching.

